Background. For a Banach space $E$ and an integer $n\ge 1$, write $E^n$ for the $n$-fold direct sum. A multi-norm on the family $\{E^n : n\in\mathbb{N}\}$ is a sequence of norms $\|\cdot\|_n$ on $E^n$ (with $\|x\|_1=\|x\|$) satisfying, for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$, $x_1,\dots,x_n\in E$ and permutations $\sigma$:
(A1) $\|(x_{\sigma(1)},\dots,x_{\sigma(n)})\|_n=\|(x_1,\dots,x_n)\|_n$;
(A2) $\|(\alpha_1 x_1,\dots,\alpha_n x_n)\|_n \le (\max_i|\alpha_i|)\,\|(x_1,\dots,x_n)\|_n$;
(A3) $\|(x_1,\dots,x_{n-1},0)\|_n = \|(x_1,\dots,x_{n-1})\|_{n-1}$;
(A4) $\|(x_1,\dots,x_{n-1},x_{n-1})\|_n=\|(x_1,\dots,x_{n-1})\|_{n-1}$.

Fix $1\le p\le\infty$ and let $p'$ be its conjugate exponent. A $p$-multi-norm on $\{E^n\}$ is a sequence of norms $\|\cdot\|_n$ on $E^n$ satisfying (A1), (A3), and the $p$-versions of the remaining axioms (so that $p=\infty$ recovers the ordinary multi-norm and $p=1$ recovers the dual multi-norm). There is a known duality $E\mapsto E'$ which, starting from a multi-norm on $E$, produces a dual multi-norm on $E'$; for general $p$, the standard duality sends a $p$-multi-norm to a $p'$-multi-norm. There is also a known "internal" duality theory for ordinary multi-norms (the case $p=\infty$) which, applied to a multi-norm, produces another multi-norm rather than a dual multi-norm.

Question. For $1\le p\le\infty$, does there exist an analogous duality theory for $p$-multi-norms whose output is again a $p$-multi-norm on the dual space (rather than a $p'$-multi-norm)?

